{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c7e9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0789a-315e-7c93-91ec-e79f4e2ce50f"},"sequence":591,"recorded_at":1780531400001177,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"991286c9-5106-4a42-ba8b-579d37a173bb","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"991286c9-5106-4a42-ba8b-579d37a173bb","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"991286c9-5106-4a42-ba8b-579d37a173bb"},"call_id":"call_01a0789ce9297150af83671cc862549c","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-24-1\",\n  \"command\": \"sed -n '60,160p' /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/fable-consolidated-review.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Read A3 A4 review text\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"Close by (before or at landing of story 2): add ownership groups for `internal/provider` and `internal/provhost`, rewrite the now-false §5.5/§7.2/§7.3/§7.5 gap texts, re-pin `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`; story 1 should either register its §7.A code or state the deferral in the §7.A gap. Longer-term (advisory): give tracecheck a reverse direction — e.g. require every `internal/*` package to appear in at least one ownership row or an explicit exclusion list — so the next unregistered package cannot land silently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### A1 — ADVISORY (structural, needs a decision before the next story): two identity/shape pipelines now exist for the same three terminal schemas\\n\\n`canonicaljson` is the designated owner of object identity: it has the trusted schema->self-field contract table (validated against the generated catalog, `canonical.go:103-152`, with `SelfManifestID`/`SelfProbeID`/`SelfEvidenceID` at `canonical.go:56,70,72`) and registers all three terminal schemas — currently as refuse-everything placeholders (`closed_shapes.go:115,130,132`). Story 1 built a second, private pipeline: `objectIdentity`/`checkIdentity` (`manifest.go:515,537`) plus full shape validation, bypassing `canonicaljson`'s strict decode (B3), AX safe-integer gate, and 5 MiB bound.\\n\\nToday the two owners give opposite verdicts on every valid terminal manifest: `terminalbackend.ParseManifest` accepts what `canonicaljson.CalculateObjectIdentity` refuses (\\\"unsupported\\\"). Whichever future code path reaches for the repo's documented identity entry point will disagree with the registry. Also note the safe-integer ordering: `checkIdentity` runs `jcs.Transform` (which rounds unsafe integers, SPEC.md:302 NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND) *before* member-type validation refuses the document — no wrong accept is reachable, but rejection happens after identity calculation, which the fixture wording forbids.\\n\\nClose by: one owner. Either implement the three closed shapes inside `canonicaljson` and have `terminalbackend` parse via `VerifyObjectIdentity`, or formally transfer ownership (remove/redirect the placeholder registrations and record it in the ownership registry). Do not leave both.\\n\\n### A2 — ADVISORY: `provhost.CheckIdentity` duplicates `canonicaljson.validateProviderIdentityRecord` rule-for-rule\\n\\n`internal/provhost/identity.go:90` vs `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go:207`: same 16-member set, same UUIDv7/subject==session rules, same bounds (1..128/1..256/1..512), same kind enum, same Antigravity realm rule, same opaque-map rules, byte-identical regexes (`identityKeyPattern` == `providerIdentityKeyPattern`, `windowsDrivePattern` == `windowsAbsolutePathPattern`). They already differ in decoder strictness (B3) and in error surface (provider_protocol_error vs identity error). provhost additionally skips `record_id` recomputation with a stated bound (\\\"content addressing belongs to the object-identity story\\\") — the correct deferral — but then re-validates everything else instead of delegating. Two validators for `urn:ax:schema:provider-identity 1.0.0` will drift.\\n\\nClose by: have `CheckIdentity` delegate the record-shape half to `canonicaljson` (mapping its error into the §15.1 provider-stdio refusal), keeping only the wants-this-provider check local; or add a bidirectional agreement test driving both validators over one fixture corpus.\\n\\n### A3 — ADVISORY: Provider Protocol 3.0.0 is pinned into v0.5.0 and no package claims it — the union has no owner for the §7.A envelope\\n\\n`internal/catalog/catalog_gen.go:190-204`: every provider operation carries `ContractVersions: [\\\"2.0.0\\\", \\\"3.0.0\\\"]`, `NormativeSection: \\\"7.2, 7.5, 7.A\\\"`, both released in v0.5.0. `internal/provhost/protocol.go:30` pins `ProtocolMajor = 2` and `protocol.go:449` classifies any 3.x envelope as `incompatible_protocol` (correct for a v2 call). §7.A binds Structured Error **1.3.0** for v3; `protocol.go:381` binds major 2 -> Error 1.0.0 only. provhost's otherwise meticulous stated-bounds paragraph (`doc.go`) never mentions v3. The only §7.A production code in either branch is `terminalbackend.CheckProviderDescriptor` — in the terminal package, incomplete (B1), and with no possible caller in provhost.\\n\\nThis is the \\\"two independently excellent packages that disagree with each other\\\" case: story 1 believes §7.A is its scope (package doc says so); story 2 built the only component that could ever speak v3 and scoped it out silently.\\n\\nClose by: a decision, not necessarily code — name the owner of the v3 envelope (Error 1.3.0 binding, descriptor carriage in launch/resume bodies), record it in provhost's stated bounds and the ownership registry, and move/complete `CheckProviderDescriptor` accordingly.\\n\\n### A4 — ADVISORY: three error models; `internal/provider`'s cannot compose into the wire layer as built\\n\\n- provhost mints wire-ready `axerror` Structured Errors, dynamic facts only in `Details` — matches `axerror`'s gates.\\n- terminalbackend mints its package `*Error` with catalog wire codes and static details — composable.\\n- provider mints its own `Error` whose `detail` deliberately embeds canonical filesystem paths, provider IDs, and owner identities (`provider.go:77-97`, `trustCandidate`, `Verify`), and whose `Error()` appends `cause.Error()`. The repo convention everywhere else (scalar `ValidationError` — \\\"without echoing the input, which may be a machine-local path\\\"; terminalbackend; provhost) is the opposite. When `cmd/ax` lifts a discovery failure into a Structured Error, a message built from `provider.Error.Error()` with the cause attached will be refused by `axerror.refuseCausalLeak` (`details.go:202-215` — message reproduces the rendered cause verbatim), so the seam will not compose without rework. Not shown to violate §15.1 itself (its excluded classes are env/stderr/bundle keys), so advisory.\\n\\nClose by: either restructure provider's constructors to carry paths in a details map (provhost pattern) now, or record the composition contract (who strips the cause, who builds the wire message) in provider's doc before `cmd/ax` exists.\\n\\n### A5 — ADVISORY: the six-provider set exists as two hand-written literals bridged only by the spec pin\\n\\n`internal/provider/provider.go` `builtinOrder` (derived-checked against §7.1 lines 2622-2653 by `enumeration_test.go:63`) and `internal/provhost/profile.go` `profileProviders` (derived-checked against the §7.7 table lines 3431-3436 by `closed_vocabulary_census_test.go:367`). While the pin holds, green suites cannot diverge — the agreement is real but transitive through SPEC.md line windows. No test asserts `provider.Builtins()` equals provhost's registry directly, and provhost's census explicitly scopes `profileProviders` out (\\\"nothing in production consults it\\\" — `ProfileMapping` consults `profileYOLOMapping` instead). One cross-package equality test at integration closes this structurally for the next spec bump.\\n\\n### A6 — ADVISORY: inventory machinery is now 20 hand-rolled AST walkers across 10 packages; these two Stories added 7\\n\\nFiles containing `go/parser` in test code: 20 (10 in canonicaljson/cliresult/config/localstore/specdoc pre-existing; provider adds 3, provhost adds 3, terminalbackend adds 1). Three distinct architectures for the same idea:\\n\\n- terminalbackend (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`): derived arms vs declared rows, resolution is **textual** (a row passes if the named test's source mentions the detail); self-documents that a dead arm can resolve through a sibling.\\n- provhost (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go:73-165`, floor 162 arms): derived arms vs witnesses, and `TestEveryArmWitnessRefusesAtTheProductionEntry` **executes** every witness through the production entry point and requires the refusal to come from that arm.\\n- provider (`refusal_inventory_test.go:31-62`): runtime instrumentation — constructor vars swapped in `TestMain`, exercised file:line set audited against AST-derived sites.\\n\\nTwo consequences. First, the weaker bound is the real bound: story 1's textual resolution proves declaration, not exercise; the executed-witness direction provhost built is strictly stronger and portable. Second, each walker re-decides \\\"what is a production file / what counts as a refusal\\\" independently, and each closes a different blind-spot set (terminalbackend rejects constructor aliasing outright; provider catches aliases at runtime; provhost's ctor-derivation keys on identifier names and relies on its separate call-site audit for aliases). Recommend extracting a shared test-support core (production-file glob + fail-closed parse + both-direction check harness) before an 8th copy appears, and porting executed witnesses to terminalbackend.\\n\\n### A7 — ADVISORY: two trust-establishment paths for external executables, with different fact sets, and nothing cross-checks them\\n\\n`terminalbackend.DigestFile` (`terminalbackend.go:603`): EvalSymlinks + regular-file + sha256, no owner check (§6.5 trust = digest + enabled). `provider.trustCandidate` (`provider.go:394`): canonicalize + inspect + **owner policy