`DecodeProbe` would remove the coupling.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Checked and found sound\\n\\n- **Wire-code membership**: every code minted by all three packages is in the pinned catalog — `terminal_backend_*` (incl. `terminal_backend_integrity_failure` at Error 1.3.0, `catalog_gen.go:545`), `invalid_config` / `local_precondition_failed` / `integrity_failure` / `provider_timeout` / `provider_protocol_error` / `provider_process_failed` / `incompatible_protocol` / `idempotency_mismatch` (`catalog_gen.go:445-479`).\\n- **Grammar agreement where duplicated**: provhost's `providerIDPattern` == `scalar`'s; semver regexes identical everywhere; provhost's hand-written platform vocabularies equal `scalar`'s set and are census-derived from the spec; terminalbackend routes platforms through `scalar.ParsePlatform`.\\n- **Digest convention**: everything flows through `scalar.ParseDigest`/`SHA256Digest`; `DigestFile`'s `\\\"sha256:\\\"+hex` output parses; `provider.Verify` compares constant-time.\\n- **Version majors**: Terminal Backend Protocol major 1 (story 1) vs Provider Protocol major 2 (story 2) vs Configuration 3.0.0 — consistent with catalog contract pins; the two Kind vocabularies (`builtin_go/local_program/trusted_executable/native_runtime` §4.B vs `builtin/external` §7.1) are distinct spec vocabularies, not a divergence.\\n- **Spec pin**: `specpin`, `specdoc`, `axerror`, `scalar`, `canonicaljson`, `catalog` are byte-identical across the two worktrees; both Stories derive vocabularies from the same `SPEC.md` bytes, so all census/derivation tests measure the same document.\\n- **provhost transport** (`runner.go`): drain-before-wait pipe ownership, process-group kill with `WaitDelay`, capped reads sized so length checks stay exact, EPIPE-race handling that prefers a judgeable frame over a write error — read closely, no defect found.\\n- **terminalbackend Reconcile** (`manifest.go:1511-1859`): deterministic check order, dedup-before-signature is safe (first instance verified, equ