ainingContract, data []byte) (*Error, error) {\\n105-\\tversion, err := BindingFor(contract)\\n106-\\tif err != nil {\\n107-\\t\\treturn nil, err\\n108-\\t}\\n109-\\treturn Decode(version, data)\\n110-}\\n111-\\n112-// requireCommonDataModel refuses bytes that are outside the Section 1.6 common\\n113-// logical data model before any member of the document is read.\\n114-//\\n115-// It exists because a duplicate member has no single value. Section 1.6 states\\n116-// the rule twice and in both directions, quoted verbatim from the pinned\\n117-// internal/specdoc/SPEC.md at the lines named:\\n118-//\\n119-//\\tSPEC.md:218 \\\"map keys MUST be UTF-8 strings and MUST be unique\\\"\\n120-//\\tSPEC.md:221 \\\"floating-point numbers, NaN, Infinity, non-string map keys, and duplicate keys are forbidden\\\"\\n121-//\\n122-// encoding/json does not refuse such a document:\\n123-// it resolves repeats, and it resolves them differently depending on the decode\\n124-// target. A map decode keeps the last occurrence, a struct decode keeps the last\\n125-// occurrence, and a decode into a map-typed member such as details merges both\\n126-// occurrences into one map. So a Structured Error carrying \\\"retryable\\\": false\\n127-// followed by \\\"retryable\\\": true used to be read as retryable - a forged retry\\n128-// claim assembled out of two members neither of which a conforming writer could\\n129-// have emitted - and a repeated details resolved to the union of both\\n130-// occurrences, which is neither one. Two conforming readers of the same bytes\\n131-// disagreed, which is precisely the compatibility property this package exists\\n132-// to hold.\\n133-//\\n134-// The gate is the same one the CLI Result reader runs (cliresult.Decode), so\\n135-// both branches of a machine reading now enforce the same data model rather than\\n136-// one of them relying on the other to do it. It reaches every peer-supplied\\n137-// envelope through DecodeBound: provider, bridge, RPC, session-adapter and\\n138-// terminal-backend payloads are read here, so the duplicate-member shape is\\n139-// remotely reachable and is refused at the same place for all of them.\\n140-//\\n141-// Canonicalize's strict decoder settles more than duplicates - malformed UTF-8,\\n142-// lone or unpaired surrogate escapes, unescaped control characters in strings,\\n143-// trailing data after the top-level value, and more than 256 simultaneously open\\n144-// containers. Measured against the mutant that deletes this call, it is the only\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 3309,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 828,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}}
